\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You have three good options, and I think one of them is better than simply abandoning the joke.\
The 2009 message is so uncannily compatible with 
\f1\i \strokec2 Clones of Elvis
\f0\i0 \strokec2  that you could 
\f2\b embrace the coincidence
\f0\b0 . Fred Mason of Roberts Creek really did send, as part of Australia\'92s 
\f1\i \strokec2 Hello from Earth
\f0\i0 \strokec2  project:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'93Have you got all our missing socks? Love, Earth.\'94\
It was transmitted from Canberra toward Gliese 581d on 28 August 2009. ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2019-11-14/hello-from-earth-10-years-on-interstellar-message/11701586?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 ABC News}})\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f2\b\fs28 1. Keep your message, acknowledge Fred Mason\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 I think this is entirely defensible artistically, especially if you independently arrived at it. Your use is materially different: the question is incoming, encrypted, apparently interdimensional, and later becomes evidence that the senders possess a recognisably similar material culture.\
And yes, you can absolutely be pleased that an Australian got there first. In fact, there is something wonderfully 
\f1\i \strokec2 Clones
\f0\i0 \strokec2 -compatible about discovering that an Australian had already used humanity\'92s first serious attempt at interstellar messaging to ask aliens about the laundry.\
I would acknowledge it 
\f2\b at the back of the novel
\f0\b0 , rather than in a foreword. A foreword risks giving away the punchline hundreds of pages before it arrives.\
Something like:\
After writing the message 
\f2\b HAS ANYONE OUT THERE FOUND OUR MISSING SOCKS?
\f0\b0 , I discovered that in 2009 Australian Fred Mason had already sent almost exactly the same question into space as part of the 
\f1\i \strokec2 Hello from Earth
\f0\i0 \strokec2 project:\
\'93Have you got all our missing socks? Love, Earth.\'94\
The message was transmitted from Canberra towards Gliese 581d.\
I am therefore unable to claim the missing-socks question as an original contribution to interstellar diplomacy.\
I am, however, extremely proud that an Australian got there first.\
That feels to me much better than a defensive attribution. It turns the collision into another joke.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f2\b\fs28 2. Make the coincidence canonical to the multiverse\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 This could be even better, provided you don't interrupt 
\f2\b Missing Socks itself
\f0\b0 . Somewhere later, perhaps in an authorial diversion, you could observe that another universe had already transmitted almost the same question in 2009.\
The lovely implication is that 
\f2\b missing socks are not merely common across universes; asking the cosmos about them is also convergent behaviour
\f0\b0 .\
You could even make Fred Mason's message one of those inexplicable correspondences between realities:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 In one comparatively backward universe, an Australian named Fred Mason had transmitted essentially the same question into space in 2009. Nobody noticed the significance, principally because nothing answered.\strokec2 \
That would turn the real event into evidence for the novel's cosmology.\
I wouldn't put this immediately after the received message, though. It would steal oxygen from Eloise's later realisation that 
\f1\i \strokec2 encryption + socks
\f0\i0 \strokec2  means these beings are disturbingly like us.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f2\b\fs28 3. Change the wording but preserve the discovery\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 If you want more distance from Mason while retaining the exact conceptual payload, these seem strongest:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 DO YOUR WASHING MACHINES LOSE SOCKS TOO?
\f0\b0 \
