\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 My preference is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 DO YOUR WASHING MACHINES LOSE SOCKS TOO?
\f0\b0 \strokec2 \
because it actually strengthens Eloise's later inference. It explicitly gives the other civilisation 
\f2\b washing machines
\f0\b0 , rather than requiring her to infer laundering technology from missing socks.\
But your present:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 HAS ANYONE OUT THERE FOUND OUR MISSING SOCKS?
\f0\b0 \strokec2 \
has something the alternatives don't: it sounds exactly like somebody has finally become desperate enough to ask 
\f2\b the entire multiverse
\f0\b0 .\
So I would be inclined to 
\f2\b keep it and acknowledge Mason
\f0\b0 . The historical coincidence is not embarrassing. It is almost suspiciously on-theme: an Australian already threw the missing-socks question into the cosmos seventeen years before you put it into 
\f1\i \strokec2 Clones of Elvis
\f0\i0 \strokec2 . ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2019-11-14/hello-from-earth-10-years-on-interstellar-message/11701586?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 ABC News}})\
